> putting the sheet at the "end of the body" (a) already works (in Chromium and mostly Gecko so far) and (b) hopefully makes them think more about the implications in a more intuitive way.

It may often work, but there's a problem with moving stylesheets to the footer: it can break the expected CSS cascade. I haven't seen this discussed so far. I'm working on improving performance in WordPress core (e.g. https://github.com/WordPress/performance/issues/2076#issuecomment-3050293295), and one area I've been looking at is deprioritizing styles for blocks that aren't in any initial viewport. For example, there is a "Social Links" block and its stylesheet is quite large, so having it as a blocking stylesheet in the `head` is problematic when the block only appears in the page footer. Moving it to the end of the `body` fixes this issue, but it causes the order of CSS rules to change. 

In WordPress, as surely in many CMSes, a site owner can add their own CSS overrides which appear at after other stylesheets have been printed in the `head`. So they may something like:

```css
.wp-block-social-links .wp-block-social-link.wp-social-link .wp-block-social-link-anchor {
    color: lime;
}
```

This will have the desired style applied when the Social Links stylesheet is printed previously in the `head`, but this user CSS rule gets unexpectedly overridden when the stylesheet is moved to the `body` by this core CSS rule:

```css
.wp-block-social-links .wp-block-social-link.wp-social-link .wp-block-social-link-anchor {
    color: currentColor;
    fill: currentColor;
}
```

Original | Stylesheet Moved to `body`
--|--
<img width="515" height="213" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0425f89a-554b-4b0d-b193-46965ab8af68" /> | <img width="516" height="211" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7bba9f28-29cc-47de-8b51-41ee9fb2b36b" />

This makes it unreliable to move a stylesheet from the default `head` placement to the end of the `body`, even though a page analysis may indicate this is better for performance since the bulk of the CSS is not needed until the footer is scrolled into view. It means optimizations cannot be applied automatically without manual testing to ensure the cascade of CSS rules doesn't break. The print media hack does not have this problem since the [order of the cascade is preserved](https://github.com/WordPress/performance/issues/2076#issuecomment-3053634747) (h/t @eric-michel).
